# fixedbugs, defect 2253, 2026-10-29: a function whose frame is larger than
# what is left of the stack died at 139 with nothing on stderr. Where the
# frame did not fit, Darwin's probe routine, `___chkstk_darwin`, faulted the
# guard on purpose while `sp` stood megabytes above it, and the guard claimed
# a fault only within a mebibyte of `sp`; Linux's clang probed nothing at all
# and moved `sp` past the guard in one instruction; and at `-O2` the
# program's `main` was inlined into C's, whose prologue probed the frame
# before `hero_args_set` installed the guard. The routine is now a witness
# (`runtime/parts/stack.c`), a Linux target compiles with
# `-fstack-clash-protection` (`selfhost/cli/flags.hero`), and C's `main` is
# compiled as written (`runtime/heroes_runtime.h`).
#
# The frame is `main`'s own, so nothing is printed before it fails. Its record
# is the header's, 128 MiB, past every leg's stack (the header says why it is
# C's struct and not records of records: those kept clang at `-O2` past ten
# minutes). Darwin and Linux name `fixedbugs-2253-...-in-main.main`; Windows'
# guard names no function and says the first sentence alone.
extern "fixedbugs-2253-held.h"
    record Held tag heroes_held partial
        first: i64
    function heroes_held_first(h: Held) -> i64

function main()
    held = Held(first: 7, rest: zero)
    print(heroes_held_first(h: held))
